Here is the rewrite of the essay into English, followed by an analysis of the author’s main point.

### English Rewrite

Three hours ago, I got off work, went home, and logged on to the internet, where I saw people mourning the passing of #YuYingshi. At that moment, all I knew was that he was the idol of the famous blogger, Professor #WangWeixiong. Beyond that, I knew nothing about his life.

However, in the 21st century, you don’t always need to consult a #teacher; with Google, you can know everything under the sun. After doing some digging, I increasingly understood why he was so famous and why Professor Wang liked to mention Old Yu so much...

Old Yu was a professor at three Ivy League schools! Isn’t that impressive? Just that title alone is enough to scare people!

I started digging into Yu Yingshi’s background like a stalker: How did the Chinese Wikipedia describe him? How did the English Wikipedia describe him? How did people praise him when he won awards? And how did the obituaries describe him today? A figure emerged in my mind whom I respect most... Teacher Yun Gen. They really are quite similar!

Unfortunately, Teacher Gen didn’t have a chair at Harvard University, but their traditional Chinese skills of being #erudite-in-antiquity-and-modernity and #identifying-as-China are definitely of the same lineage. Teacher Gen likes Daoism and Buddhism, while Old Yu seems to be a fan of Confucianism.

But that’s where the trouble lies.

Confucianism emphasizes benevolence, righteousness, propriety, wisdom, and faithfulness; it values the order of elders and juniors, and the proper conduct of rulers and ministers. But if you studied at a top US university and just got your PhD during the peak of the hippie era, surely you wouldn’t really go around teaching people to #respect-teachers-and-follow-the-way and to stop with all that #individualism, right? That would get you in trouble.

By the way, how scholars avoid getting in trouble is a lesson Confucius paid for with a lifetime of bitterness, and Confucian disciples couldn’t possibly not know it: In Confucius’s time, the realm was on the verge of chaos. He traveled among the states, hoping to secure a minor official position to do something great. But he only knew how to recite the rites and laws of the Duke of Zhou; he didn’t know how to cater to his bosses’ needs, and in the end, he returned with nothing. However, failure is the mother of success, and his students progressed much further. The Grand Historian said: “The disciples of the seventy scattered and roamed among the feudal lords; the great ones became tutors and high ministers, while the lesser ones became friends and teachers of the scholar-officials.” Why?

Because after several generations of transmission, they learned to read the monarch’s facial expressions. By the time of Mencius, his interactions with the kings were different; there was banter and laughter, and he knew how to flatter the boss: “King of Qi, I see you can’t even bear to kill a cow, so you must be a benevolent ruler! If you follow the Way of the King, you will succeed!”

By the late Warring States period, the “Confucian” strategists had mastered the game of “interviewing the monarch” to perfection. To get official positions, they discarded “benevolence” and “propriety” and all went to study the new technology of “Rule of Law.” So, Xunzi, as a Confucian disciple, taught students who could become high officials in the Qin Shi Huang government. You certainly wouldn’t detect a shred of Confucius’s spirit in Li Si.

Today, the United Nations has over a hundred or two hundred countries, and the situation is more complex than in the Spring and Autumn period. Confucian heirs left China, this (ancient) land of propriety, and went to the barbaric land of the military hegemon, the United States. Surely they wouldn’t really pull out a copy of the *Analects* and tell people to #observe-three-years-of-mourning, right? Everyone is familiar with how humiliating it was when Confucius and his students were shut out by various states. So, once in America, Confucius’s disciples must say that the Master also supports #democracy, that #democracy-is-great, and that the Communists are scum who ruin the family and the nation. Only then can they become Ivy League professors!

Someone might ask: Do these words have no consequences? Of course not! Confucius and Mencius died over two thousand years ago, and the Confucian tradition was smashed to pieces during the #MayFourthMovement. As the academic authority at a top university in the only superpower after WWII, if you said that Johnny Kung teaches people to take drugs and play with women, some people might actually believe it. After all, the US #theoretical-nukes are solid facts; the consequences of the Japanese not believing in the supernatural are still vivid in our minds from back then.

So why could Old Yu back then just pick a few sentences from the *Book of Han*, translate them into English, and get a PhD, with several universities hiring him as a professor? #YouKnowWhatIMean. They push you onto the altar to be an authority so that you can firmly say: “Everyone here is a duck (a fool), you think Confucius’s thoughts are feudal? Oh, what a big misunderstanding! Actually, he was a fighter for democracy! If you don’t have a Harvard chair, just listen quietly!”

As for whether Confucian thought conflicts with democracy? Honestly, do these “Sinologists” know anything? You say: The Republic of China is an example. Hey, big brother, is the political system in Taiwan really “Confucian”? The KMT heads curse Tsai Ing-wen every day; is that how Confucianism works? Did Tsai Ing-wen perform elaborate rituals to worship heaven and earth? No. The scientific spirit, not being a charlatan, goes against the Confucian ethos. You think using Chinese characters makes it Confucian? You think Confucianism can only exist in China?

As I mentioned earlier, Japan is Confucian, and the UK is Confucian (at least among the world’s advanced nations, they are the most similar to Confucianism): The monarch speaks of etiquette and grandeur, power is granted by heaven, supreme and untouchable, everyone is in hierarchical layers, no challenging across levels, and only then is society stable. As for the successes and failures of their democratic systems, it is not for an Americanized Chinese person to have the right to comment.

***

### Commentary and Main Point

Disregarding the heavy sarcasm, profanity, and colloquial tone, the author’s main argument is a critique of **academic opportunism and the distortion of Confucianism to fit Western liberal democratic narratives.**

Specifically, the author conveys the following points:

1.  **Confucianism is fundamentally incompatible with modern liberal democracy:** The author argues that traditional Confucianism is hierarchical, authoritarian, and focused on social order, propriety, and the relationship between ruler and subject. It is not naturally aligned with concepts like individualism, equality, or democratic rights.
2.  **Yu Yingshi (and similar scholars) engaged in "survival" adaptation:** The author suggests that for Chinese scholars to succeed in the Western academic establishment (specifically Ivy League universities), they had to "sell" Confucianism as a precursor or ally of democracy. This was a strategic move to gain legitimacy and employment in the West, rather than a genuine philosophical truth.
3.  **The "Ivy League" credential is a tool of authority:** The author mocks the idea that having a Harvard or Ivy League title automatically grants someone the authority to define what Confucianism is. He implies that these scholars are "authorities" only because the Western system elevated them, and they in turn validate the system by claiming Confucius was a "democratic fighter."
4.  **Critique of "Sinology" and Western-centric views:** The author is skeptical of Western "Sinologists" (or Chinese scholars working in the West) who claim to understand the nuances of Chinese culture. He argues that they often misinterpret or flatten complex traditions to make them palatable to Western audiences.
5.  **A broader geopolitical/cultural observation:** The author uses the example of Yu Yingshi to make a larger point about how non-Western cultures are often reinterpreted or "domesticated" to fit Western ideological frameworks (like democracy) in order to be accepted in global academic and political discourse. He contrasts this with his view that true "Confucian" societies (like Japan or the UK, in his ironic framing) maintain strict hierarchies and do not necessarily equate their systems with liberal democracy.

In essence, the author is arguing that **Yu Yingshi’s academic success was built on a convenient, perhaps misleading, narrative that Confucianism is compatible with democracy, a narrative that served the interests of both the scholar (gaining Western acceptance) and the Western academic establishment (validating its own ideological superiority).** [end of text]


